- Confirm current HF medications and optimal doses. Ask whether any BP changes would affect HF meds or require adjustment.  
- Ask about monitoring for volume status (weight, edema), signs of worsening HF, and whether BNP or echocardiogram updates are needed.

Bone health / osteopenia — questions and tests
- “When was my last DEXA? What were the T-scores at spine and hip? How often should I repeat it?” (Typical repeat interval for osteopenia is 1–3 years depending on risk.)  
- “Given my PPI (omeprazole) use, does that change fracture risk or the type of calcium I should take?” (PPIs are associated with modestly increased fracture risk; consider calcium citrate if absorption issues.)  
- “Is my current calcium & vitamin D dosing adequate? Do I need a 25-hydroxyvitamin D level?”  
- “Should we calculate my FRAX 10-year fracture risk? Does it change management?”  
- “Do I meet criteria for starting pharmacologic therapy (bisphosphonate or other agent)?” (Reasons to treat: T-score ≤ −2.5, fragility fracture, or FRAX risk above treatment threshold.)  
- “Any additional labs to screen for secondary causes?” — consider: CMP (calcium, creatinine), 25-OH vitamin D, TSH, CBC, albumin, and if indicated PTH, testosterone (in men), or 24-hr urine calcium/PTH for hyperparathyroidism.

When to consider a referral to Endocrinology
Referrals are reasonable if any of the following apply:
- T-score ≤ −2.5 (osteoporosis) or prior low-trauma fragility fracture.  
- Rapid bone loss on serial DEXA or fractures while on treatment.  
- Unexplained or severe biochemical abnormalities (high calcium, very low vitamin D despite replacement, abnormal PTH) or suspected endocrine cause (hyperparathyroidism, Cushing).  
- Complex cases where you and PCP are considering initiation of second-line osteoporosis medications (denosumab, teriparatide, romosozumab) or have intolerance to bisphosphonates.  
- If you want a specialist opinion about long-term PPI use and bone risk or for evaluation of other endocrine issues (thyroid, adrenal, metabolic bone disease).

If none of those apply, your PCP can often manage osteopenia (DEXA surveillance, calcium/vitamin D, lifestyle, consider bisphosphonate if risk rises). But if you prefer a specialist consult for one-time assessment, referral is reasonable.

Specific items you might ask Dr. Nguyen to order now
- BMP (creatinine, potassium), CMP (calcium), TSH, 25-hydroxyvitamin D, lipid panel, A1c (if diabetes risk), and urine albumin/creatinine ratio.  
- DEXA scan if >2 years since prior or if you don’t have a recent result.  
- FRAX calculation and documentation of T-scores.  
- Echocardiogram or cardiology follow-up if it’s been a while or if symptoms changed.

Questions to phrase succinctly during the visit
- “Given my HF and home BP readings, is my current BP goal and medication regimen still appropriate?”  
- “Do you recommend any changes now, or should we monitor home BPs and reconvene?”  
- “Should I get labs or imaging today (DEXA, BMP, vitamin D) and what results would prompt endocrinology referral?”  
- “Does my omeprazole affect my calcium absorption or bone health, and should I change formulation/timing?”

Peak flow note / clarification
- You said your last peak flow was 28. Can you confirm the units and context (e.g., 280 L/min, or percent predicted)? A numeric peak flow of 28 as written sounds unusually low; I want to be sure I understand because that could be important for pulmonary/cardiac risk assessment.

Red flags to bring to urgent attention
- New or worsening shortness of breath, sudden weight gain, fainting or near-fainting, chest pain, very low BP, syncope, or new fractures — contact provider/ER as appropriate.

Would you like:
- A short, printable checklist/one-page script you can bring to the appointment with the top 6 questions?  
- Help drafting a message to Dr. Nguyen (email or portal) asking for these labs and a visit agenda?

Also please confirm: sex (M/F), current blood pressure medicines and doses, date and T-scores from your last DEXA (if known), how long you’ve been on omeprazole, and clarify that peak flow number. With those I can tailor the checklist and suggested wording.

