I can help — here’s a focused checklist and suggested questions to take to your annual physical (and a short referral request you can use if you want to ask Dr. Nguyen for an endocrine opinion). I’m not a clinician, so treat this as general guidance and please discuss with your PCP before making changes.

Quick clarifying questions before I draft anything or go further:
- Do you mean peak expiratory flow of “28” L/min, or 2.8 L/sec (or something else)? That number looks unusual without units.
- What is your sex? (I see “Vince” in earlier memory but want to confirm for screening recommendations.)
- What hypertension medications (if any) are you currently taking?
- When was your last DXA scan and do you have the T-scores/results? Any fragility fractures?

Questions and points to raise with Dr. David Nguyen (PCP) about hypertension management
- Current status and targets
  - “What should my blood-pressure target be given my history of heart failure?” (Many clinicians target <130/80 for patients with HF, but the target can vary by HF type and frailty.)
- Recent measurements & monitoring
  - “Can we review my home BP log or set up ambulatory BP monitoring to confirm control?”
  - “Should I check orthostatic BPs (lying/sitting/standing) given my age/heart-failure meds?”
- Medication review & interactions
  - “Which antihypertensive would you recommend given my heart-failure history and other meds?” (ACEi/ARB/ARNI and certain beta-blockers are often indicated in HF; diuretics, MRAs, etc., have implications for electrolytes.)
  - “Are there drug interactions with omeprazole, calcium, or other meds I take that we should worry about?”
  - “If I’m on a loop diuretic, should we worry about bone loss or low calcium?”
- Safety and monitoring
  - “How often should we check BMP (kidneys, electrolytes), potassium, and eGFR after med changes?”
  - “Should we monitor BNP, ECG, or echocardiogram frequency as part of BP/HF follow-up?”
- Practical items
  - “Any lifestyle changes (salt, weight, exercise) you want me to prioritize?”
  - “If BP is not at target, what is your plan for stepwise medication adjustment or referral to cardiology?”

Things to ask or have available about your bone health and whether endocrinology referral makes sense
- Key facts to provide or ask your PCP to review
  - Date and results of last DXA (T-scores at spine, hip, femoral neck) and any history of fragility fracture.
  - Current supplements: calcium dose and vitamin D dose; type of calcium (carbonate vs citrate).
  - PPI use: mention omeprazole 20 mg daily and ask whether this affects calcium absorption or bone risk.
  - Any steroids or other meds that affect bone, family history of osteoporosis/hip fracture, smoking/alcohol, falls.
- Labs and assessments to request (if not recently done)
  - CMP (including serum calcium, creatinine), 25-OH vitamin D, TSH, and possibly PTH if calcium abnormal.
  - Consider fasting labs or A1c as part of general risk assessment.
  - If concern for secondary causes: serum protein electrophoresis, morning cortisol, testosterone (men), or other targeted tests as indicated.
  - FRAX 10-year fracture-risk calculation (with and without BMD).
- When to ask for endocrinology
  - Appropriate reasons to request referral:
    - Very low bone density (T-score ≤ -2.5) or a fragility fracture.
    - Rapid bone loss on serial DXA despite supplements.
    - Suspected secondary causes of osteoporosis (e.g., hyperparathyroidism, severe vitamin D deficiency, unusual lab findings).
    - Complicated treatment decisions (e.g., intolerance or contraindication to bisphosphonates, planning pregnancy, need for anabolic therapy).
  - If your DXA shows osteopenia only and there are no red flags, PCPs often manage initially (lifestyle, calcium/Vit D, exercise, consider FRAX and possibly start medication depending on risk). But referral is reasonable if you want a specialist opinion given age + heart history + PPI use.
- Bone-specific therapy considerations to discuss
  - Would a bisphosphonate, denosumab, or other agent be appropriate given your overall cardiovascular/renal status?
  - If on omeprazole, ask about which calcium formulation to use — calcium citrate is less dependent on stomach acid than calcium carbonate.
  - Consider fall-risk assessment and fall-prevention measures.

Suggested labs/imaging to request at the annual visit
- DXA scan (if not done within 1–2 years or if you haven’t had one recently)
- 25-OH vitamin D, CMP (including calcium, creatinine), TSH
- FRAX calculation (with BMD)
- BMP/electrolytes and A1c/lipids as part of general health and HF management

Short script you can use to ask Dr. Nguyen for an endocrinology referral (edit as you like)
“Hi Dr. Nguyen — I’m due for my annual physical and want to review blood pressure and bone health. I’m 55 with osteopenia (on calcium + vitamin D), take omeprazole 20 mg daily, and have a history of heart failure. Could you:
1) Review whether my BP target/management should be adjusted given my HF and current meds and order BP monitoring and necessary labs (BMP, eGFR, potassium)?
2) Review my most recent DXA and labs and consider a referral to Phoenix Endocrinology if you think a specialist evaluation for secondary causes of bone loss or advanced bone therapy is warranted?
Thanks — I can bring recent records or DXA results to the visit.”

