- Any recent lab results, prior DXA report, and dates of prior cardiac testing (echo, BNP, ECG)
- A short symptom list: dizziness/orthostasis, lightheadedness, shortness of breath, falls, new back/height loss, fractures

Hypertension management — questions to ask and items to cover
- “Given my history of heart failure, what blood pressure target should I aim for?” (In many patients with HF and without contraindications, a target around <130/80 mmHg is often recommended if tolerated, but individualization is important.)
- “Are my current BP readings and medications achieving that target? Should we change doses or medications?” (Bring med list.)
- “Should I keep a home BP log or start ambulatory BP monitoring to check for masked or white-coat hypertension?”
- “Should we check orthostatic vitals today or review for symptoms of low BP when standing?”
- Tests to request related to BP/HF: BMP (creatinine, electrolytes), spot urine albumin/creatinine ratio, ECG (if not recent), lipid panel, A1c; consider BNP or an echocardiogram if HF symptoms or if it’s been >12 months or you’ve had changes.
- Medication-safety points: check renal function and potassium after dose changes (important with ACEi/ARBs, MRAs). Discuss side effects (fatigue, dizziness).

Bone health (osteopenia) — what to ask and do
- “When was my last DXA and what were the T-scores? Do I need a repeat DXA now?”
- “Based on my T-scores and FRAX (10-year fracture risk), do I meet criteria for starting pharmacologic therapy (bisphosphonate, etc.)?”
- Ask for 25-hydroxy vitamin D and serum calcium (and PTH if calcium abnormal) if not recent.
- Because you’re on omeprazole: mention it and ask if calcium citrate would be preferable to calcium carbonate (calcium citrate is better absorbed with PPI use).
- Review PPI necessity — long-term PPI use is associated with a small increased fracture risk; ask about stepping down if appropriate.
- Tests to consider: DXA if not recent (frequency usually every 1–2 years if change expected or therapy started), vitamin D level, basic metabolic panel, and possibly PTH if calcium abnormal.
- Referral triggers to Phoenix Endocrinology or bone specialist:
  - T-score ≤ -2.5 (osteoporosis) or new fragility fracture
  - Rapid bone loss on repeat DXA despite treatment
  - Concern for secondary osteoporosis (abnormal labs: very low/high calcium, abnormal PTH, thyroid disease, Cushing’s)
  - Complex cases or intolerance to first-line therapy
  - Young age with unexpectedly low bone density or unusual lab findings
If none of the above apply and your DXA/FRAX are low-moderate risk, your PCP can often manage with lifestyle, supplements, and primary osteoporosis meds. An endocrinology referral is reasonable if the PCP finds abnormal labs or if you meet the criteria above.

Other medication and interaction notes (short)
- Omeprazole can reduce absorption of calcium carbonate and possibly increase fracture risk slightly; calcium citrate is preferred with chronic PPI.
- If a bisphosphonate is considered, follow administration instructions (take with water, remain upright, separate from calcium by at least 2 hours).
- Check kidney function before starting some osteoporosis medications (e.g., IV bisphosphonates have renal cautions).

Pulmonary note about “peak flow 28”
- Please clarify what test/units “28” refers to (peak expiratory flow L/min, percent predicted, FEV1 liters?). If you have COPD/asthma or low lung function, mention symptoms; PCP may repeat spirometry or refer to pulmonary if indicated.

Suggested concise script/questions to bring to Dr. Nguyen (copy-paste)
- “Given my heart failure, what average blood pressure target should I be trying to achieve? Is <130/80 appropriate for me?”
- “Here is my home BP log — do we need to adjust medications or check for orthostatic hypotension?”
- “Which labs should we run today for blood pressure and heart-failure monitoring (BMP, potassium, urine albumin, BNP)?”
- “When was my last DXA and what were my T-scores? Do I need a repeat DXA or FRAX assessment now?”
- “I take omeprazole and calcium — should I switch to calcium citrate, and is my PPI still necessary?”
- “Do I meet criteria for starting osteoporosis medication or for referral to endocrinology?”
- “Do any of my symptoms or recent test results suggest I need a cardiology or endocrinology referral now?”

Follow-up options I can do for you
- Draft a ready-to-send message to Dr. Nguyen summarizing concerns and asking for specific tests/referrals.
- Draft a backup email (so you can send it to your secondary address) or create a draft the assistant can save for you.
- Help interpret specific lab/DXA/echo results if you share them.

Tell me:
1) If you want me to draft an email to Dr. Nguyen (and any extra details to include), and
2) Clarify the “peak flow 28” and your current BP readings and meds (if available) — then I’ll draft the visit note and suggested orders you can bring or send.

