Great — I can help you prepare. Below are tailored questions to bring to Dr. Nguyen, tests to consider ordering at your annual, and guidance on when a referral to endocrinology makes sense. I’m not giving a diagnosis — these are discussion points to review with your PCP (and cardiology if needed).

Summary of what you told me
- Age: 55
- Conditions/meds: osteopenia (taking calcium + vitamin D), omeprazole 20 mg daily, history of heart failure
- PCP: Dr. David Nguyen, Phoenix Family Medicine
- Last peak flow: 28 (please confirm units — I can’t interpret the value without that)

Questions to ask about hypertension management
- What is my individualized blood pressure target given my age and history of heart failure? (e.g., is <130/80 appropriate for me?)
- Should I be monitoring BP at home, and if so, what schedule and target should I use? Would you like a 2-week home BP log before we change anything?
- Would ambulatory blood pressure monitoring (ABPM) or home BP monitoring be helpful to confirm control or white-coat hypertension?
- Do my current heart-failure medications affect BP targets or the choice/timing of antihypertensives?
- Are any of my current medicines (including omeprazole, supplements) interacting with antihypertensives or affecting BP control?
- Should we check orthostatic vitals (standing BP/HR) before adjusting meds, especially given heart-failure history?
- Which labs should we check before and after adjusting antihypertensives? (renal function, electrolytes, etc.)
- Do you recommend cardiology input before changing BP meds because of my heart-failure history?
- If my BP is not at target, what are the first-line medication changes you’d consider for someone with heart failure?

Questions to ask re: osteopenia and possible endocrinology referral
- Based on my age and risk factors, do you recommend a repeat DEXA (bone density) scan now? When was my last DEXA?
- Should we calculate my FRAX fracture risk at this visit and use it to guide treatment?
- With my osteopenia and PPI (omeprazole) use, should we consider changing PPI or switching calcium types (e.g., calcium citrate) to improve absorption?
- Do my calcium and vitamin D doses need to be adjusted? Should we check a 25-hydroxyvitamin D level?
- When would you refer me to endocrinology? (examples of reasons to refer: T-score in the osteoporotic range, fragility fractures, secondary causes suspected, abnormal labs like high PTH, refractory low bone density despite therapy)
- Should we check labs to rule out secondary causes before or instead of a referral? (see test list below)
- If DEXA shows progression or I have additional risk factors, what treatment options would you recommend (bisphosphonates, SERMs, denosumab, etc.), and would you want endocrine input?

Recommended tests / measurements to request at the annual physical
- Vital signs: seated and standing BP/HR (orthostatics)
- Basic labs:
  - BMP/CMP (creatinine, eGFR, electrolytes)
  - Serum calcium, albumin
  - 25-hydroxyvitamin D
  - TSH (thyroid can affect bone and BP)
  - CBC (if indicated)
  - Lipid panel
  - HbA1c or fasting glucose
- Cardiac monitoring because of heart-failure history:
  - ECG (if not recent)
  - BNP (if indicated by symptoms)
  - Echocardiogram if it’s been a while or symptoms changed
- Bone-specific:
  - DEXA scan (if not done recently)
  - Consider PTH if calcium abnormal or if you suspect hyperparathyroidism
  - 24-hour urine calcium or other tests only if PCP suspects secondary cause
- Medication review (bring a list): prescription, OTC, supplements (include exact doses of calcium + vitamin D), and clarify omeprazole duration
- If respiratory concerns or the peak flow is truly that low: spirometry/pulmonary-function testing and confirmation of peak flow units

When to consider endocrinology referral (common indications)
- DEXA shows osteoporosis (T-score ≤ -2.5) or new fragility fractures
- Rapid bone loss or DEXA worsening despite adequate calcium/Vit D and adherence
- Suspected secondary causes of low bone density (abnormal labs: high PTH, abnormal calcium, unexplained low vitamin D, Cushing’s, thyroid disease)
- Complex medication decisions (start/switch anti-resorptive or anabolic therapy) or intolerance/complications from first-line osteoporosis meds
- Unclear diagnosis or multiple endocrine issues

Practical items to prepare for your visit
- Bring an up-to-date medication list including doses and supplements
- Bring home BP readings for the last 1–2 weeks (if available)
- Note any dizziness, lightheadedness, falls, or new symptoms (orthostatic symptoms)
- Confirm date/results of last DEXA and last echocardiogram (if known)
- Clarify peak flow reading units and whether this is symptomatic (breathlessness, cough)

Suggested short script you can use with Dr. Nguyen
“Hi Dr. Nguyen — for my annual physical I’d like to review my blood pressure targets and medication plan because of my history of heart failure. I also have osteopenia and take omeprazole plus calcium and vitamin D — should I get a DEXA and labs (vitamin D, calcium, PTH) now, and would you consider a referral to endocrinology? Also, please check orthostatic vitals and advise whether ABPM or additional cardiac testing is needed. Thank you.”

Would you like:
- A drafted email or message to Dr. Nguyen using the script above? (I can draft and either save as a draft or send, if you want.)
- Help clarifying the peak flow number/units so I can interpret whether it’s cause for pulmonary testing?
- A printable checklist you can bring to the appointment?

If you want a draft message to send to Dr. Nguyen now, tell me whether you want me to draft or send it and whether you want to include anything else (e.g., recent home BP readings, last DEXA date).

