I can help — below is a focused checklist and suggested questions to use at your annual physical with Dr. David Nguyen, plus guidance on when an endocrinology referral is usually appropriate. Before I do anything else: would you like me to draft an email or message to Dr. Nguyen requesting specific labs, a DEXA, or a referral? I can draft that for you when you say “Yes — draft an email.”

Quick clarifying questions for you (so I can personalize recommendations or a draft):
- What blood pressure medications (if any) are you currently taking and what are your recent home or clinic BP readings?
- When was your last DEXA and what were the T-scores (spine/hip)? Any fragility fractures?
- Do you have diabetes, thyroid disease, or are you on chronic steroids?
- Any recent labs (BMP, eGFR, calcium, vitamin D, PTH, A1c, lipids)?
- What do you mean by “last peak flow was 28”? (units and context — were you measuring for asthma/COPD?)

Hypertension — questions to ask Dr. Nguyen
- Based on my age (55) and heart-failure history, what is my current BP target?
- Are my home BP readings adequate? (Show him a log if you have one.) Should I start ambulatory or home BP monitoring?
- Are any of my current heart-failure medications also controlling BP? Should any med be adjusted to better address both HF and hypertension?
- Do you recommend any change to my antihypertensive regimen given my kidney function and potassium?
- Are there symptoms I should watch for (dizziness, lightheadedness, orthostatic symptoms) that would require dose changes?
- Should we check basic labs now to monitor meds: BMP (creatinine, eGFR, potassium), and an ECG if not recently done?
- If my BP remains elevated despite 3 meds including a diuretic, when would you investigate resistant HTN and consider secondary causes (sleep apnea, primary aldosteronism, renal artery disease)?
- Lifestyle: any specific sodium, fluid, weight monitoring guidance given my HF history?

Suggested baseline tests to review for BP / HF management
- Basic Metabolic Panel (creatinine, eGFR, potassium)
- BNP (if not done recently) or NT-proBNP — useful for heart-failure status
- ECG (if not recent)
- Review of current med list, weights and edema trend, and daily weights if on diuretics

Osteopenia / endocrinology referral — what to ask and consider
- Ask Dr. Nguyen:
  - When was my last DEXA scan and should we repeat it now?
  - What is my FRAX fracture risk score and does it meet treatment thresholds?
  - Is my current supplement regimen (calcium + vitamin D) appropriate in dose and timing, especially while taking omeprazole?
  - Do you recommend endocrine evaluation for secondary causes of bone loss?
  - Should I stop or taper omeprazole or review indications for it given possible effects on bone health?
- Common reasons to refer to endocrinology for bone health:
  - T-score ≤ −2.5 (osteoporosis) or a prior low-trauma (fragility) fracture
  - Rapid bone loss despite supplements or therapy
  - Abnormal labs suggesting endocrine causes (e.g., high PTH/hyperparathyroidism, very low vitamin D refractory to replacement, hyperthyroidism, Cushing’s, low testosterone in men)
  - Complex medication decisions (e.g., starting bisphosphonates, denosumab, or anabolic agents), especially with comorbid conditions (heart failure, renal impairment)
  - Men with osteoporosis or younger-than-typical onset
- Labs to consider for bone assessment:
  - 25-hydroxy vitamin D
  - Serum calcium, phosphorus
  - Parathyroid hormone (PTH) if calcium abnormal or poor response to supplements
  - Basic metabolic panel (renal function affects treatment choices)
  - TSH (thyroid dysfunction can affect bone)
  - Consider 24-hour urine calcium if hypercalciuria suspected

Notes on omeprazole and bone health
- Long-term proton pump inhibitor use has been associated with slightly increased fracture risk in some studies and may impair calcium absorption for certain calcium salts. It’s reasonable to review whether omeprazole is still needed and, if so, to discuss calcium formulation/timing (e.g., calcium citrate is less dependent on stomach acid than calcium carbonate).
- Any medication changes should be coordinated with Dr. Nguyen — don’t stop PPIs abruptly.

Putting it together — suggested appointment plan
- Bring an up-to-date medication list (prescription + OTC supplements).
- Bring home BP log (if available).
- Ask for these orders before/at the visit if not done recently: BMP (creatinine, potassium), 25-OH vitamin D, calcium, PTH (if indicated), TSH, A1c (if diabetic risk), lipid panel, DEXA scan (if >2 years since last or if no recent DEXA).
- Discuss BP targets and med plan considering HF; ask whether referral to nephrology or a hypertension specialist is needed only if BP is resistant or there are complex renal/electrolyte issues.
- Discuss whether endocrinology referral is recommended — likely not required if your osteopenia is stable on supplements and DEXA / labs are unremarkable, but appropriate if your DEXA shows osteoporosis, you have fractures, abnormal labs, or complex treatment needs.

Would you like:
- A concise one-page checklist to print and bring to the visit?
- A drafted email or message to Dr. Nguyen requesting specific labs, a DEXA, and asking whether an endocrinology referral is recommended? (If yes, tell me which items you want included and whether to CC anyone.)

I can draft either the checklist or the email now — tell me which and provide any of the clarifying details above if you want the draft personalized.

