I can help — below is a focused prep checklist plus suggested questions to bring to your annual physical with Dr. David Nguyen, and guidance on when an endocrinology referral is commonly appropriate. I’m not a doctor, so treat this as general information to help you discuss things with your PCP.

Pre-visit checklist (bring or have ready)
- Current medication list (names, doses, frequency): include omeprazole 20 mg, calcium + vitamin D (exact doses and formulation), all heart-failure meds, OTCs, supplements.
- Home blood pressure log (last 2–4 weeks: date/time/BP, posture, symptoms). If you don’t have one, bring a plan to start measuring morning and evening for 1–2 weeks.
- Most recent labs and imaging (BMP/CMP, lipid panel, A1c if done recently, 25‑OH vitamin D, any prior DEXA scan reports and dates).
- Any recent weight change, shortness of breath, edema, chest pain, lightheadedness, falls, fractures, or medication side effects.
- Clarify your “peak flow 28” — what units/scale and date (that number sounds unusually low depending on units; ask your PCP to interpret).

Questions to ask about hypertension management
- What is my blood-pressure goal given my age (55) and history of heart failure? (Different guidelines target <130/80 in many patients but individualization is needed with heart failure.)
- Are my current BP readings at home consistent with clinic readings? Do you recommend home monitoring or ambulatory BP monitoring?
- Based on my current meds and heart failure, should any antihypertensive be started, stopped, or adjusted? Which classes would you prefer (ACEi/ARB, beta blocker, diuretic, etc.) and why?
- How do my heart-failure medications affect BP targets or choice of antihypertensive?
- Do I need any safety monitoring if we change BP meds (renal function, potassium, orthostatic checks)?
